WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.338
(synthesizer)

00:00:02.338 --> 00:00:03.171
- [Narrator] Milk,

00:00:03.171 --> 00:00:06.737
it's the elixir all mammals
feed to their young.

00:00:06.737 --> 00:00:09.455
Deep inside the National
Zoo in Washington, D.C.,

00:00:09.455 --> 00:00:11.334
a team of scientists are devoted

00:00:11.334 --> 00:00:13.417
to unlocking the wonders of the white gold

00:00:13.417 --> 00:00:16.478
to help animals like baby Fiona,

00:00:16.478 --> 00:00:18.087
but more on that later.

00:00:18.087 --> 00:00:20.077
Welcome to the world's largest collection

00:00:20.077 --> 00:00:21.910
of exotic animal milk.

00:00:24.756 --> 00:00:26.007
- OK, let's get to it.

00:00:26.007 --> 00:00:27.852
- [Narrator] Meet Doctor Michael Power.

00:00:27.852 --> 00:00:28.716
Can I call you Mike?

00:00:28.716 --> 00:00:29.549
- Sure.

00:00:29.549 --> 00:00:30.382
- [Narrator] OK, Mike.

00:00:30.382 --> 00:00:31.215
What do you do here?

00:00:31.215 --> 00:00:34.897
- I'm the curator of the
Exotic Animal Milk Repository

00:00:34.897 --> 00:00:36.825
at the Smithsonian's National Zoo.

00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:39.289
It's the largest, by a species count,

00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:41.952
number of samples of
milk in the entire world.

00:00:41.952 --> 00:00:43.939
(light music)

00:00:43.939 --> 00:00:45.747
We have samples that have been collected

00:00:45.747 --> 00:00:47.128
from all over the world

00:00:47.128 --> 00:00:49.650
that we keep in frozen storage.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:50.997
- [Narrator] Animals like?

00:00:50.997 --> 00:00:53.685
- Armadillos, bats, giraffe,

00:00:53.685 --> 00:00:54.901
giant ant eater,

00:00:54.901 --> 00:00:56.389
Asian elephant,

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:57.949
African lion,

00:00:57.949 --> 00:00:58.951
cheetahs,

00:00:58.951 --> 00:01:01.088
tigers, sea lions,

00:01:01.088 --> 00:01:01.921
and zebra.

00:01:04.348 --> 00:01:05.341
- [Narrator] I hate to ask,

00:01:05.341 --> 00:01:07.199
but how do you get the milk?

00:01:07.199 --> 00:01:09.825
- [Michael] Well I'll Batang show you.

00:01:09.825 --> 00:01:12.802
Batang is a critically
endangered Bornean orangutan.

00:01:12.802 --> 00:01:14.580
So this is the classic collaboration

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:16.885
between a scientist and
the animal care staff.

00:01:16.885 --> 00:01:18.658
Their incredible work
and effort to be able

00:01:18.658 --> 00:01:22.035
to training the animals to be
able to accept this procedure

00:01:22.035 --> 00:01:25.452
to where they voluntarily donate samples.

00:01:30.008 --> 00:01:31.413
- [Narrator] So now that we know the how,

00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:32.364
we have to ask,

00:01:32.364 --> 00:01:33.197
why?

00:01:33.197 --> 00:01:37.123
- The exotic mammal milk
repository serves two

00:01:37.123 --> 00:01:38.150
critical purposes.

00:01:38.150 --> 00:01:41.081
One is basic science and
simply trying to understand,

00:01:41.081 --> 00:01:43.122
in a sense, the origin of milk,

00:01:43.122 --> 00:01:45.420
and the other is a very
practical, pragmatic purpose

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.309
in the fact that we have to
occasionally hand-rear animals,

00:01:48.309 --> 00:01:49.551
and therefore we have to come up

00:01:49.551 --> 00:01:51.178
with a milk replacer formula.

00:01:51.178 --> 00:01:52.011
- [Narrator] OK,

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:53.590
so when a baby can't
be fed by their mother,

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:55.504
you guys help come up with the formula

00:01:55.504 --> 00:01:58.021
that's as close to the
mother's milk as possible.

00:01:58.021 --> 00:01:59.437
- Have you heard about baby Fiona

00:01:59.437 --> 00:02:00.627
at the Cincinnati Zoo?

00:02:00.627 --> 00:02:01.460
(TV static)

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:02.469
- Is the baby hippo,

00:02:02.469 --> 00:02:05.711
who has stolen the hearts of
people all over the country.

00:02:05.711 --> 00:02:08.511
- Baby Fiona was born six weeks premature.

00:02:08.511 --> 00:02:10.234
She's a Nile hippo,

00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:14.401
and no other baby hippo
hand been hand-raised

00:02:14.501 --> 00:02:15.819
in North America.

00:02:15.819 --> 00:02:17.969
And so we needed to put
together a milk formula

00:02:17.969 --> 00:02:20.001
to be able to feed her to have her grow.

00:02:20.001 --> 00:02:21.164
So we worked together

00:02:21.164 --> 00:02:24.984
with the Exotic Animal Milk
Repository at the National Zoo

00:02:24.984 --> 00:02:28.125
to come up with a formula
to feed baby Fiona.

00:02:28.125 --> 00:02:29.545
- [Narrator] So how's Fiona doing now?

00:02:29.545 --> 00:02:31.699
- [Barbara] Milk is almost
removed from her diet.

00:02:31.699 --> 00:02:34.229
We are very happy that baby
Fiona is happy and healthy,

00:02:34.229 --> 00:02:35.143
and growing very well.

00:02:35.143 --> 00:02:35.981
- [Narrator] Awesome.

00:02:35.981 --> 00:02:37.561
Back to Mike.

00:02:37.561 --> 00:02:38.905
- So, what's next?

00:02:38.905 --> 00:02:42.101
- The milk repository has
samples from over 180 species

00:02:42.101 --> 00:02:43.947
of mammals and that
sounds pretty impressive,

00:02:43.947 --> 00:02:45.808
until you realize that there's anywhere

00:02:45.808 --> 00:02:48.149
from 5,000 to 6,000 species of mammals.

00:02:48.149 --> 00:02:51.997
So we have only sampled
maybe 3% of the mammals.

00:02:51.997 --> 00:02:54.943
It's still only a tiny tip of the iceberg

00:02:54.943 --> 00:02:57.114
compared to what we could be learning.

00:02:57.114 --> 00:02:59.614
(light music)

